{"name":"Changing Seasons","hex":"#b6927c","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"changing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of change"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Change; alteration."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/changing"},{"term":"seasons","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of season"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of season"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seasons"}],"entries":[{"title":"Changing Seasons (music video)","blocks":[{"text":"Changing Seasons is a music video by Indian composer A. R. Rahman. The video was simultaneously released in Hindi and Tamil on 18 January 2011. The song \"Jaare Udd Jaare\" (Hindi) / \"Naan Varuvene\" (Tamil) featured in the video was composed by Rahman and performed by Rahman and Jali Fily Cissokho. The video, produced by Y. M. Movies and filmed by John Warner, features Aishwarya Rai along with Rahman. The song was originally composed for Bollywood film Raavan and its Tamil version Raavanan. The song was previously released on the Hindi Raavan and the Tamil Raavanan soundtrack albums respectively in 2010."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Changing_Seasons_(music_video)","wikidata":"Q5072030"},{"title":"Season","blocks":[{"text":"A season is a division of the year based on changes in weather, ecology, and the number of daylight hours in a given region. On Earth, seasons are the result of the axial parallelism of Earth's tilted orbit around the Sun. In temperate and polar regions, the seasons are marked by changes in the intensity of sunlight that reaches the Earth's surface, variations of which may cause animals to undergo hibernation or to migrate, and plants to be dormant. Various cultures define the number and nature of seasons based on regional variations, and as such there are a number of both modern and historical definitions of the seasons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png/330px-ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Season","wikidata":"Q24384"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png/500px-ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png/1280px-ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png/500px-ChangingSeasons_NH_01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The astronomical and meteorological periods of the four-seasons reckoning across the year at the Northern Hemisphere, displayed as segments of Earth's orbit. Astronomically a season encompasses the time between an equinox and solstice. Meteorologically seasons are quarters of the annual temperature cycle,[1] centered on the season characteristic temperatures, which trail equinox and solstice.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChangingSeasons_NH_01.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/North_season.jpg/500px-North_season.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/North_season.jpg/1280px-North_season.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/North_season.jpg/500px-North_season.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Axial parallelism is a characteristic of the Earth (and most other orbiting bodies in space) in which the direction of the axis remains parallel to itself throughout its orbit.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANorth_season.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Earth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN_-_corrected.png/120px-Earth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN_-_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Earth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN_-_corrected.png/250px-Earth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN_-_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Earth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN_-_corrected.png/120px-Earth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN_-_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEarth-lighting-summer-solstice_EN_-_corrected.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Earth-lighting-winter-solstice_EN.png/120px-Earth-lighting-winter-solstice_EN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Earth-lighting-winter-solstice_EN.png/250px-Earth-lighting-winter-solstice_EN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Earth-lighting-winter-solstice_EN.png/120px-Earth-lighting-winter-solstice_EN.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEarth-lighting-winter-solstice_EN.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Earth_seasons_2021-2022.jpg/120px-Earth_seasons_2021-2022.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Earth_seasons_2021-2022.jpg/250px-Earth_seasons_2021-2022.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Earth_seasons_2021-2022.jpg/120px-Earth_seasons_2021-2022.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEarth_seasons_2021-2022.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/ReflectedSolarRadiation_Solstices.jpg/120px-ReflectedSolarRadiation_Solstices.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/ReflectedSolarRadiation_Solstices.jpg/250px-ReflectedSolarRadiation_Solstices.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/ReflectedSolarRadiation_Solstices.jpg/120px-ReflectedSolarRadiation_Solstices.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReflectedSolarRadiation_Solstices.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/The_Sun_at_perihelion_and_aphelion.jpg/500px-The_Sun_at_perihelion_and_aphelion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/The_Sun_at_perihelion_and_aphelion.jpg/1280px-The_Sun_at_perihelion_and_aphelion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/The_Sun_at_perihelion_and_aphelion.jpg/500px-The_Sun_at_perihelion_and_aphelion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Angular size of the Sun at Earth's orbital periapsis called perihelion (max./closest, 2. to 5. January) and at apoapsis called aphelion (min./furthest, 3. to 6. July). The resulting 7% difference in solar intensity does not overpower geographic factors of climate, with seasons staying more moderate on the Southern Hemisphere due to having less landmass.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Sun_at_perihelion_and_aphelion.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Hemisphere_land.png/500px-Hemisphere_land.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Hemisphere_land.png/1280px-Hemisphere_land.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Hemisphere_land.png/500px-Hemisphere_land.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHemisphere_land.png"}],"sounds":{"changing":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Persent101-changing.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/81/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Persent101-changing.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Persent101-changing.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/81/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Persent101-changing.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Persent101-changing.wav.ogg"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"1995 UAZ 2206 beige rear","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/1995_UAZ_2206_beige_rear.jpg/500px-1995_UAZ_2206_beige_rear.jpg","ratio":1.2959381044487428,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1995_UAZ_2206_beige_rear.jpg","share":0.1525},{"title":"Beige and brown oil-paper umbrella on the edge of a wooden house in Luang Prabang Laos","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Beige_and_brown_oil-paper_umbrella_on_the_edge_of_a_wooden_house_in_Luang_Prabang_Laos.j